WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.036 line:0%
-I'm so happy to meet you,
and thank you so much

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.204 line:0%
for coming on the show.

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:06.139 line:0%
You hit a home run
with this movie, buddy.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:08.842 line:0%
I can't even tell you
what a great job you did.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.677
We saw it.
We screened it the other night.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.978
You're fantastic
in this movie.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.846
-Thank you.
-Congratulations.

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:15.348
It was so good.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.682
I want to get down to all
the details

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:19.119
and ask you all the questions.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:20.987
Here's some of the reviews,
by the way, just so you know,

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:22.555
if you want to plug your ears.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:26.226
But one says that, "When
Austin Butler shakes his hips

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:29.028
in Elvis's first gig
as a full blown rock and roller,

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:32.665
it's like watching
two stars being born."

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:35.001
That was time out.
I like that one.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.570
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.639
"Vanity Fair" says,
"It may well be

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:42.141
a star making turn for
Austin Butler."

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:44.410
And I agree with that.
And then this one's my favorite.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:45.711
"Entertainment Weekly" says.

00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:49.882
"Austin Butler stares down
the lens and melts it."

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:54.887
I've never heard that. Melted.

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.756
But I did feel that when I was
watching the thing --

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:59.726
Baz Lurhmann did such an amazing
job directing.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:02.362
-Yeah, he did. I'm so proud
of everything he's done.

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:03.763
-Yeah.
-It was incredible.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.965
-Did you feel -- When you were
auditioning, when you knew

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:08.134
that there's an audition
for "Elvis" and you're like,

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.502
"I'm close to it,"
did you get nervous?

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:10.770
Did you always know Elvis?

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.138
-Yeah.
-You did?

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.439
-Yeah, it's terrifying.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.542
But it's that thing
where it's --

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:19.078
I mean, it's the biggest
responsibility I've ever felt.

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:22.314
You feel such a responsibility
to him, to his family,

00:01:22.314 --> 00:01:24.850
to all the people around
the world who love him so much.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.253
And so, it's terrifying.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.956
But it's also the thing
where as an actor --

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:32.058
like, the actors that I always
looked up to when I was a kid,

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.594
my dad always had Turner Classic
Movies in the house,

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:38.798
and so "Raging Bull"
and "East of Eden"

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:40.800
and "On the Waterfront"
and all those,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.102
so, I was always like,
"What did they do?"

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:45.004
And so, it's always been
about the challenge.

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:48.174
So, I leaned into that
as much as I could.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:50.643
-But then once you got cast
as Elvis,

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.845
do you go like, "Okay,
now I got the gig.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:54.847
Now it's really hard.
I really got to get to work"?

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:56.816
-You feel like you're about
to climb Mount Everest.

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:58.183
-Yeah, it really is.

00:01:58.183 --> 00:02:00.052
And you did it perfectly.

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:02.054
And I can't even tell you
you didn't do too much.

00:02:02.054 --> 00:02:03.056
It wasn't too little.

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:04.657
Wait until you seen this
performance.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.325
I'm telling you, buddy,
it was a knockout.

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:09.762
I mean, just even the accents
that Elvis had,

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.731
the way he talked
in the '50s,

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.599
the way he talked in the '60s
and '70s,

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.935
like three different Elvises.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:19.171
And you sang differently. And
you actually sang in this movie.

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:20.373
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.575
Well,
I'm saying that because --

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.044
Sorry. But I mean, we've had
some actors on the show.

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.247
They played parts in movies
and they don't sing

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:30.049
and they lip sync,
but you can play guitar

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.751
and you sang and you dance
and move just like him.

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:34.887
But I mean,
how did you do the accents?

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:36.088
How did you remember
what you were doing?

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:38.123
-Well, you know, I mean,
for one thing,

00:02:38.123 --> 00:02:40.492
you listen to Elvis speak

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:42.595
and there's these amazing
archives out there

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.862
of every interview he ever gave.

00:02:43.862 --> 00:02:46.065
And that was the first thing
that hit me, was

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:48.634
it's not
just one voice of Elvis.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:51.337
His voice changed so much
over the years.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.405
And so it's that thing of...

00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:55.108
-Could you could you give us
an example?

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:56.742
I'm sorry to put you
on the spot.

00:02:56.742 --> 00:02:59.978
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:59.978 --> 00:03:01.748
-Alright, alright.
I'll do it.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.749
So, I mean, I haven't
done this at all,

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:08.120
and it's been a long time,
so take it easy on me, Internet.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.123
Alright, so, like
an example is

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:12.158
when he was 19

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:13.625
and he goes on
the "Louisiana Hayride".

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:18.063
It's 1954. And his voice --
you know, he's nervous.

00:03:18.063 --> 00:03:20.433
His voice at that time,
you speak a lot faster

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.602
when you're nervous,
and also, he was young.

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:26.405
And so, his voice at that point
was like,

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:27.539
[As Elvis] Well, I'd like to say
how happy we are

00:03:27.539 --> 00:03:29.041
to be down here. It's a real
honor for us

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:31.844
to get a chance to appear
on the "Louisiana Hayride."

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.145
[ Normal voice ]
It's like that.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:36.448
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:41.320
And then, like, a couple years
later, he's 21, it's 1956,

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.689
and honestly,
this one I thought of earlier,

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.558
because it's how
I feel right now.

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.226
It's that thing when
you're not sleeping at night,

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:48.394
you're just buzzing.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.729
I can't believe --
I got to take a second.

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:51.798
I'm on Jimmy Fallon

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.899
talking about playing
Elvis Presley.

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:55.468
Like, you're my hero.

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:57.803
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:57.803 --> 00:04:01.274
I just blows my mind.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.109
I just -- Yeah.
So, at this point --

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:08.514
So, he's 21, and at that point,
he's more like -- he says --

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:09.815
because he's asked,

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:11.050
"What are you thinking
about at night?"

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.152
And he says,
[as Elvis] "Well,

00:04:14.152 --> 00:04:16.055
everything's happened to me so
fast in the last year

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:17.757
and a half.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:19.058
I'm all mixed up, you know?

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:20.726
I can't keep up with
everything that's happening."

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:22.461
-Yeah. It's unbelievable.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:24.363
-[Normal voice]
So, there's that.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:25.364
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:27.032
And then for, like,
a juxtaposition,

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:29.202
like, we can fast forward
to '72.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.769
Like, there's this
great interview in '72,

00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:34.873
and one of the first thing
he says is...

00:04:34.873 --> 00:04:36.275
[as Elvis] Well --
because at this point,

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:39.345
his voice goes more forward
into his face you know,

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.714
and so, then he's like,

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:45.217
"Well, you know,
we just came out here

00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:48.054
from Memphis," [normal voice]
and that sort of thing.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:49.188
-Yea, it's coming out
of the front.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:50.422
-It comes out of the front
of your face.

00:04:50.422 --> 00:04:51.624
-It's like three
different characters.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.793
It's such a great love
letter to Elvis

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:56.462
and any Elvis fans.
Dude, ultimate respect.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:05:00.065
And I was reading some article
about this and you were saying

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:03.035
what kind of hit home to you is
his love for his mother.

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:05.671
-Yeah. Yeah.
-And now he lost his mom.

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:07.807
-Yeah.
That was the first thing,

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.575
because when I first
started approaching it,

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:12.712
it's that thing of Elvis
is always 40 feet tall.

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:15.147
He's like --
you could only look up to him.

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.817
And it's hard to, in the
beginning, to feel anything

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:21.687
but small compared to him,
and all those questions.

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:23.255
You know, I'm a shy person.

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:27.593
I feel very, you know --
those moments of "Am I enough?"

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.829
and imposter syndrome
and all those things.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.764
And so, it was a process
in the beginning

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:33.865
of just trying
to find his humanity.

00:05:33.865 --> 00:05:38.004
And I tried many things,
but I watched this documentary,

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.639
and I learned that his mom
passed away when he was 23.

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:43.609
And that's how old I was
when I lost my mom.

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:45.377
And that hit me
like a freight train,

00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:47.946
because it's the most
human thing.

00:05:47.946 --> 00:05:50.182
Like, that grief that you feel.

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:53.185
And I know you've experienced,
you know, that feeling.

00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:55.254
And that's the thing
where it just humanizes somebody

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:57.423
and you suddenly
don't feel alone in that.

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.425
And so, that that became
the first key

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.561
into finding his humanity.

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.096
-Yeah, you just played it
perfect.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:08.233
And you got to -- obviously,
you had to go to Graceland,

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:11.269
I'm assuming,
and meet Priscilla.

00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:12.204
-That was so surreal.

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:13.238
The first time I went,

00:06:13.238 --> 00:06:15.274
it was about a month
after I was cast.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:18.177
And Baz and I took this road
trip from Nashville to Memphis

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:20.512
and I got to record -- the first
recording studio I ever was

00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:24.383
in was RCA, where Elvis recorded
over 250-something songs.

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:25.751
You know, it's insane.

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:27.920
And so, then
we drove to Memphis.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.523
-I'm freaking out.
This is unbelievable.

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:32.725
-Also, I was just in Graceland.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:34.493
And Angie, who runs the spot,
she said,

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:36.529
"Tell Jimmy, because I know
he's a big fan,

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:38.498
tell him I will
show him everything."

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:40.199
So let's take you
in the archives, everything.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:41.200
-Really?
'Cause I've not been.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.601
Because I was waiting --

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:44.370
I want to wait for the exact
right moment to go to Graceland.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.038
-Oh, you'll get all the hookups.
-Really?

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:47.372
-Yeah, for sure.

00:06:47.372 --> 00:06:50.042
-Someone sent over this photo
of you in the Jungle Room

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:52.111
playing Elvis's guitar.

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:55.781
-Yeah. I still am waiting
for somebody to wake me up.

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.583
-I mean, wait, so how
did you end up--

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.986
-Well, like they had wanted me,

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:01.620
you know,
for social media or something.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:02.988
They were like,
"Well, we'll get you,

00:07:02.988 --> 00:07:04.957
like, playing the guitar,
playing a guitar."

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:07.927
And they were going to just
have me play some random guitar

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.628
at one point.
I kept putting it off

00:07:09.628 --> 00:07:11.764
because I'm not a big social
media person as well.

00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:13.732
And then when we got
to Memphis, they were like,

00:07:13.732 --> 00:07:14.767
"We're making you do it."

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.201
And so they got
some other guitar.

00:07:16.201 --> 00:07:17.703
And Angie heard

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:20.239
that I was going to be
playing a song, and she said,

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:21.307
"What guitar you playing?"

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:23.842
I said,
"Oh, it's a 1937 Epiphone."

00:07:23.842 --> 00:07:26.278
And she goes,
"Well, I got a 56 Gibson,"

00:07:26.278 --> 00:07:28.114
and I realize
what she's saying,

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:29.982
and this is -- I actually
didn't know this,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:32.585
but that guitar is the guitar
he played in

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:36.455
"Loving You", "Jailhouse
Rock", "King Creole".

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:38.924
It's also the guitar --
Scotty Moore had it redone.

00:07:38.924 --> 00:07:40.326
So, it's the same guitar
he plays in

00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:41.560
"That's the Way It Is.
-No way.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.062
-Same guitar he plays in Vegas.

00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:45.163
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:45.163 --> 00:07:48.567
-And the last person
to play it in the archives

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:49.768
was Paul McCartney.

00:07:49.768 --> 00:07:52.137
-Last person to play it
was Paul McCartney?

00:07:52.137 --> 00:07:55.274
Come on. What is going on?
How great is that?

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:57.810
And what was Priscilla like?
-Oh, man.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.646
She's just the loveliest person.
And when I first met her,

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.583
she said very few words,

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:05.650
but it was more
looking in our eyes.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:08.086
And you realize
this is the woman

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:10.556
that Elvis fell in love
with all those years ago,

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.624
and she still loves him
to this day.

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:14.093
She's the mother
of his only child.

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:16.195
And that was so surreal.

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:18.865
But she said
a couple of things.

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:21.166
She said,
"You have big shoes to fill."

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:24.403
And I said, "I know", and
just -- you feel the pressure.

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:26.038
And then she said --

00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:28.240
and then she gave me
this big hug and she said,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.343
"You have a lot of support."
And then that was huge.

00:08:31.343 --> 00:08:33.279
And then she said,
"Have you been a Graceland yet?"

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:35.281
I said, "I'm about to go in
for the first time."

00:08:35.281 --> 00:08:37.816
And she said, "I truly believe
that's where his spirit is."

00:08:37.816 --> 00:08:39.318
And then I went over there
for the first time

00:08:39.318 --> 00:08:41.920
and I spent the whole day
in there by myself.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:45.290
And now getting to,
you know, spend time with her

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.327
and be in the Jungle Room
and be with Lisa Marie,

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.796
it made me feel like
I was a part of the family.

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:52.498
It was so amazing.

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:55.134
Are you a dancer? I'm sorry to
ask this, because --

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:56.769
-Oh, no.
[ Chuckles ]

00:08:56.769 --> 00:08:58.971
-I don't know. I was like,
I don't know if I've seen you --

00:08:58.971 --> 00:09:01.407
-I wasn't a singer or a dancer.
-No.

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:03.742
You definitely dance in this.

00:09:03.742 --> 00:09:06.478
I mean, you're just like --
You're, like, a shy person.

00:09:06.478 --> 00:09:08.247
Like at a party,
would you --

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:09.982
-I was always a wallflower,
you know?

00:09:09.982 --> 00:09:11.417
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:13.818
But that was the thing, is you
realize that Elvis wasn't --

00:09:13.818 --> 00:09:16.288
he didn't do choreography

00:09:16.288 --> 00:09:18.357
in the way that Michael did
or something like that.

00:09:18.357 --> 00:09:21.894
It was
all the music moving him.

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:24.896
And obviously, there are
signature things that he did

00:09:24.896 --> 00:09:27.766
that, you know,
you have to be specific about.

00:09:27.766 --> 00:09:30.536
But it was all about the way
that the music moved him.

00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:32.771
-Could you show me a move?
-Oh, God.

00:09:32.771 --> 00:09:38.744
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:39.945
-If you can't --

00:09:39.945 --> 00:09:42.714
-Alright. Alright.
Get out here.

00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:46.685
It's been a long time.

00:09:46.685 --> 00:09:49.788
Alright.

00:09:49.788 --> 00:09:51.656
From the '50s,
he had this one

00:09:51.656 --> 00:09:55.394
that was really fun.
I just called it the Sidewinder

00:09:55.394 --> 00:09:59.598
'cause you can go from walking
into a side.

00:09:59.598 --> 00:10:02.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

